    "text": "Lake Formation \"not authorized\" errors despite a table grant usually come down to one of a handful of root causes \u2014 missing IAM permissions alongside the LF grant, the underlying S3 data location not being registered/granted, a resource link or cross-account catalog issue, or the query engine running under a different principal than the one you granted. To dig into the right one, I need a few specifics:\n\n- **Database and table name** (and catalog ID if it's a cross-account/shared catalog)\n- **The principal** (IAM role or user ARN) that's actually running the query \u2014 not just who you granted permission to\n- **Which query engine** you're using (Athena, Redshift Spectrum, EMR, Glue job, etc.)\n- **Region and AWS account ID** where the table lives\n\nOnce I have those, I can check the Lake Formation permissions, data location grants, and the querying principal's IAM policy to pin down what's missing."
